    "reply": "One associated record is Gene NCBIGene:1029, name: cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [1]. Another associated gene is Gene NCBIGene:11200, name: checkpoint kinase 2 [3]. A third identified gene is Gene NCBIGene:7157, name: tumor protein p53 [4].\n\n## Genetic Pathology\nLi-Fraumeni syndrome is an inherited susceptibility condition driven by specific genetic alterations [19: \"cancer predisposition syndrome caused by a germline mutation of the TP53 gene\"]. The inheritance occurs in a dominant manner with severe expression [19: \"autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance with high penetrance\"]. Individuals carrying these alterations face a substantial probability of developing various neoplasms [19: \"very high lifetime cumulative risk of developing multiple malignancies\"].\n\n## Clinical Heterogeneity\nPatients with this syndrome present with distinct forms of cancer across different tissues [19: \"tumors include soft tissue sarcomas, osteosarcoma, premenopausal breast cancer, brain tumors, and adrenal cortical carcinomas\"]. The presentation of the disease can differ significantly even among relatives sharing the same genetic alteration [22: \"Phenotypic heterogeneity is present among different families affected by the same pathogenic variant in TP53 gene and among members of the same family\"]. This diverse clinical variation may be influenced by multiple interacting molecular factors [22: \"polymorphic variants in TP53 gene or in TP53-related genes, copy number variations in particular regions, and/or epigenetic deregulation\"].",
